@meta
id: 28-09
title: Reload the config without restarting
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure, vimrc-debug
par: 138
minutes: 6
vscode: settings.json applies as you type it
open: .vimrc order.provider.ts

@brief
A vimrc is only run at startup, so a line you have just written is not
live yet. Quitting and reopening to test it is the slow loop, and the one
that makes people give up on configuring vim at all.

`:source %` runs the file you are editing. `:source $MYVIMRC` runs your
config from anywhere. Either way the loop becomes: write a line, source
it, try it, keep it or delete it — all without leaving the session.

Add this mapping to the Mappings section, with its reason on the line
above rather than beside it:

    " Y yanks to the end of the line, like D and C
    nnoremap Y y$

Then source the file and use it. In the provider buffer, `reinstate`
logs nothing useful: its `logger.info` call is missing the payload that
`cancel` has. Copy that payload — from the space before `{` to the end of
the line — onto the end of the `reinstate` call. With the mapping
sourced, `Y` takes exactly that and nothing else.

Do it without sourcing and `Y` still means `yy`, you paste a whole line,
and the file does not match. The grading tells you whether the source
happened.

@keys
| `:source %` | run the file you are editing |
| `:source $MYVIMRC` | run your config from anywhere |
| `Y` | with the mapping: yank to the end of the line |
| `t{` | move to just before the next `{` |
| `p` | put after the cursor |

@hints
Write the mapping, `:w`, then `:source %`. The mapping is live in this
vim from that moment.
--
An inline comment is not safe after `:nnoremap` — the quote becomes part
of what the mapping types. Put the mapping down first and open the
comment line above it with `O`.
--
In the provider: `t{` on the `cancel` log line puts the cursor on the
space before the brace, `Y` yanks from there to the end of the line, and
`$` then `p` on the `reinstate` line appends it.

@solution
    G o nnoremap Y y$ <Esc> O " Y yanks to the end of the line, like D and C <Esc>
    :w<Enter> :source %<Enter> :bn<Enter>
    /order.cancelled<Enter> t{ Y /reinstated<Enter> $ p :wqa<Enter>

The `:source` is three keystrokes of the hundred and the only one that
matters. Everything you write in this chapter should be tested this way
before you believe it.

Two things `:source` will not do for you. It runs your config *again* on
top of the current state, so it adds mappings and autocommands rather
than replacing them — an autocommand defined outside an `augroup` ends up
registered twice, and a mapping you deleted from the file is still in
memory until you restart. And a setting you changed by hand with `:set`
during the session is overwritten by whatever the file says, which is
occasionally a surprise and usually what you wanted.

`Y` itself is worth keeping. `D` and `C` both act to the end of the line;
`Y` is the odd one out, yanking the whole line like `yy` for historical
reasons. Vim's own defaults file has considered changing it for years.
Your config can change it today, which is rather the point of having one.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Mappings ---
nnoremap <C-l> :nohlsearch<CR><C-l>

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Mappings ---
nnoremap <C-l> :nohlsearch<CR><C-l>
" Y yanks to the end of the line, like D and C
nnoremap Y y$

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    this.logger.info('order.cancelled', { orderId: order.id });
  }

  public async reinstate(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    this.logger.info('order.reinstated',
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    this.logger.info('order.cancelled', { orderId: order.id });
  }

  public async reinstate(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    this.logger.info('order.reinstated', { orderId: order.id });
  }
}
